.Dd August 7, 2026
.Dt NETMAN 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm netman
.Nd passive network monitor with side-by-side layer 2 and layer 3 views
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -iface Ar interface
.Op Fl -pcap-file Ar file
.Op Fl -listen Ar address
.Op Fl -port Ar port
.Op Fl -fade Ar seconds
.Op Fl -static-dir Ar directory
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
captures network traffic and serves a web interface showing two views of it
side by side: Etherman, the layer 2 conversations between MAC addresses, and
Interman, the layer 3 conversations between IPv4 and IPv6 addresses.
Both views are fed by a single capture.
.Pp
Etherman places every station on one large circle, since a layer 2 network is
flat and all stations share the same segment.
Interman draws one circle per network, classful for IPv4 and /64 for IPv6,
with the networks spread over a ring.
Node size follows the cumulative byte count, edge width follows the observed
bit rate, and the colour of an edge is that of its dominant protocol.
MAC addresses are labelled with their vendor from an embedded OUI database,
and IP addresses are renamed as soon as a reverse DNS lookup succeeds.
Neither resolution ever happens on the packet path.
.Pp
.Nm
is strictly passive: it captures and displays, and never injects traffic.
.Pp
The following options are available:
.Bl -tag -width "-static-dir directory"
.It Fl -iface Ar interface
Capture interface, given either as an index in the interface list or as a
substring of an interface name.
If omitted,
.Nm
prints a numbered list of interfaces and prompts for one.
This option cannot be combined with
.Fl -pcap-file .
.It Fl -pcap-file Ar file
Replay a capture file instead of capturing live.
No network interface is opened in this mode, and no privileges are needed.
.It Fl -listen Ar address
Address the web interface listens on.
The default is
.Li 127.0.0.1 ,
which restricts access to the local machine.
.Pp
A wildcard address,
.Li 0.0.0.0
or
.Li :: ,
means every interface.
In that case
.Nm
opens one socket per address family, so that it is reachable over both IPv4
and IPv6, and prints one URL per usable local address rather than the
wildcard itself, which no browser can connect to.
IPv6 link-local addresses are left out: they require a zone index that
browsers handle unreliably.
.It Fl -port Ar port
TCP port for the HTTP and WebSocket service.
The default is
.Li 8080 .
.It Fl -fade Ar seconds
Delay after which nodes and edges that have gone silent disappear from the
views.
The default is
.Li 60 .
This can also be changed from the web interface, where it is synchronised
across all open tabs.
.It Fl -static-dir Ar directory
Directory holding the frontend files served at the root of the web interface.
The default is
.Pa static ,
relative to the current directory.
Packaged builds normally point this at the installed copy.
.El
.Sh CAPTURE PRIVILEGES
Live capture needs privileged access to the capture device.
.Pp
On Windows it requires Npcap.
Installing it with its default options is enough; the
.Dq WinPcap API-compatible
mode is not needed.
If Npcap was installed with
.Dq Restrict Npcap driver's access to Administrators only ,
.Nm
has to be run as administrator.
.Pp
On Unix systems capture goes through the packet filter device, which is
normally reserved to the super-user, so
.Nm
is run as root unless those devices have been opened up to a dedicated group.
Read access to them is enough to observe every frame reaching the interface,
so it should only be granted to trusted users.
.Pp
On macOS the
.Pa /dev/bpf*
devices are reserved to root by default.
Membership of the
.Dq access_bpf
group, created by Wireshark's ChmodBPF package, grants capture without
running
.Nm
as root.
.Pp
Replaying a capture file with
.Fl -pcap-file
needs no privileges at all.
.Pp
On a switched network only the local host's own traffic plus broadcast and
multicast is visible.
Connect the machine to a SPAN port, mirror port or TAP to observe more.
.Nm
stays passive by design and cannot work around this.
.Sh WEB INTERFACE AND WEBGL
The frontend renders both graphs with sigma.js, which requires a working
WebGL context.
Without one, the page loads but stops before opening its WebSocket, and the
status indicator stays on
.Dq connecting
indefinitely.
The symptom points at the network, but the cause is graphics: no packet is
ever requested from the server.
.Pp
This is worth knowing on any machine with no usable GPU, such as a virtual
machine or a headless server, where a browser may end up without WebGL unless
it falls back to a software implementation.
.Pp
To tell a WebGL problem from a real network problem, open the browser
developer console.
A WebGL failure shows an exception raised while the renderer is being
constructed, and no WebSocket connection is ever attempted.
Firefox also reports the state of WebGL under
.Pa about:support .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "static/" -compact
.It Pa static/
Frontend files served by the web interface, unless
.Fl -static-dir
says otherwise.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Capture on
.Li em0
and serve the interface to the local machine only:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
netman --iface em0
.Ed
.Pp
Serve it to the whole network over both IPv4 and IPv6, on a different port:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
netman --iface em0 --listen 0.0.0.0 --port 9000
.Ed
.Pp
Replay a capture file, without touching any interface:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
netman --pcap-file capture.pcap
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It The status indicator stays on "connecting"
The browser has no WebGL context.
See
.Sx WEB INTERFACE AND WEBGL .
.It Interface not found
The argument to
.Fl -iface
matched no interface.
Run
.Nm
with no option to get the numbered list.
.It Permission denied on the capture device
Run with the privileges described in
.Sx CAPTURE PRIVILEGES .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr tcpdump 1 ,
.Xr pcap 3
.Sh HISTORY
This program is
.Em netman-reborn ,
a modern recreation.
It is not the original Netman, shares no code with it, and is not maintained
by the authors of the original.
What it reproduces is the intent of those tools and their visual language.
.Pp
The Netman suite was developed at Curtin University, in Western Australia,
and dates from 1993.
Two of its tools are recreated here: Etherman, which showed layer 2
conversations between MAC addresses, and Interman, which showed layer 3
conversations between IP addresses.
Both were X11 programs, and separate ones: seeing the two views meant running
the two tools.
.Pp
The author of this recreation used the original tools in the 1990s at
T\('el\('ecom Paris, his engineering school, on Sun SPARC workstations
running SunOS 4.1.3.
.Pp
Of the two, only Etherman left a lasting descendant.
EtherApe describes itself as modelled after Etherman and carries that lineage
on.
What it did not take over is Interman's layout: one circle per network, with
the hosts of a classful IPv4 network, or of an IPv6 /64, grouped together and
the networks themselves spread over a ring.
That layout is reproduced here.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Alexandre Fenyo
